SpotStay Style & Branding Guidelines
Brand overview
We want SpotStay to feel like walking into a well-kept guesthouse: bright, calm, and clearly cared for. That's why most of the screen is soft grey and white, the text is a friendly near-black, and there's exactly one splash of color — our coral — pointing you to the thing you came to do.
If you're ever unsure about a design decision, ask yourself: would this make a host or guest feel welcome, or would it make them feel like they're filling in a form?
How we want SpotStay to feel
Welcoming. Friendly words, plenty of breathing room, soft rounded corners. Nothing sharp or shouty.
Trustworthy. People are handing us their homes and their holidays. Calm neutrals and honest, clearly labelled numbers go a long way.
Effortless. Each screen should have one obvious next step. If we need two big buttons, we probably need two screens.
Optimistic. Coral is our bit of energy. It works because we use it sparingly — the moment it's everywhere, it stops meaning anything.
Principles we design by
The content is the star. Property photos and key numbers should do the talking. Our UI is the frame, not the painting.
One accent, on purpose. Coral marks the primary action. We don't use it to decorate.
Light and flat. We get depth from white cards sitting on a grey background, not from heavy shadows or borders.
Stick to the rhythm. Every gap comes from our spacing scale. It's a small thing that makes the whole product feel put together.
Mobile isn't an afterthought. Lots of hosts manage their listings from their phones, so every layout should fold neatly into one column at 768px.
A quick note on our name: it's SpotStay — one word, capital S twice. We see "Spotstay" and "Spot Stay" creep into decks and copy all the time, so please keep an eye out for them.
Color palette
Our palette is deliberately small: six colors, and that's it. A good rule of thumb is that about 90% of any screen should be neutral (greys and white), and coral should take up well under 10%. If a screen looks "pink", something has gone wrong.
