Weights, and when we use them
400 Regular for most reading: body copy, subtitles, labels.
600 Semibold for titles and buttons. This is our "pay attention" weight.
700 Bold only for numbers. Keeping bold for stats is what makes them pop on the dashboard.
A few habits we'd love everyone to keep
Leave 0.5rem between a title and the line under it. It's small, but it keeps headings and their subtitles feeling like a pair.
Sentence case everywhere: "Add property", not "Add Property" or "ADD PROPERTY". All caps reads like shouting.
Keep paragraphs to about 75 characters per line so they're comfortable to read.
Emphasize with weight, not italics or underlines. Underlines look like links.
Layout & spacing
Everything lives in a centered container that tops out at 1400px wide, with 2rem (32px) of padding around it, sitting on our grey Mist background. We never let the page scroll sideways — if something's overflowing horizontally, that's a bug, not a feature.
Our spacing scale
All our spacing is based on 1rem = 16px. These six steps cover pretty much everything we've needed so far:
Token
Value
Where you'll see it
--space-2xs
0.5rem (8px)
Between a title and its subtitle, or a number and its label
--space-xs
0.875rem (14px)
Top and bottom padding inside buttons
--space-sm
1rem (16px)
Page padding on mobile, gap in the stacked header, icon to title
--space-md
1.5rem (24px)
Inside cards, between stat cards, button side padding
--space-lg
2rem (32px)
Page padding, gaps between sections and property cards
--space-xl
4rem (64px)
Top and bottom of empty states

If you genuinely need a value that isn't here, that's fine — just add it as a token and let the team know, rather than dropping a one-off number into the CSS.
How a typical page is put together
Header. Page title and subtitle on the left, the main button on the right, lined up at the top. 2rem of space below.
Stats row. A row of stat cards, each at least 200px wide with 1.5rem between them. Another 2rem below.
Main content. Usually the property grid. If there's nothing to show yet, the empty state goes here instead.
The grids, in plain terms
Stats use repeat(auto-fit, minmax(200px, 1fr)). The cards stretch to fill the row, so four stats on a wide screen look balanced.
Properties use repeat(auto-fill, minmax(320px, 1fr)) with a 2rem gap. The difference matters: auto-fill keeps empty columns, so if a host only has two listings, their cards stay a normal size instead of stretching across the whole page.
Embedded content like maps or calendars (.iframe-section) goes full width with 2rem above and below.
Components
Pretty much every SpotStay component is either a white card or a coral button: rounded corners, no border, no shadow. The specs below come straight from our production CSS, so if you spot a mismatch between this page and the app, let us know — one of them is wrong.
Primary button
This is the most important component we have, so it gets the most detail.
Property
Value
Background
#FF385C, darkening to #E61E4D on hover
Text
White, 1rem, weight 600
Padding
0.875rem top/bottom, 1.5rem left/right (14px × 24px)
Corners
8px radius
Border
None
On hover
Darkens and lifts 1px (translateY(-1px))
On click
Settles back down (translateY(0))
Transition
all 0.2s ease

That tiny 1px lift on hover is one of our favorite details. It makes the button feel physical without being showy.
One per screen. On desktop it sits top-right in the header.
Labels are short, friendly verbs in sentence case: "Add property", "Book now", "Save changes". Avoid "Submit" and "OK" — they don't tell anyone what happens next.
Secondary buttons aren't coral. We don't have a secondary button in the CSS yet. Until we do, our suggestion is a white button with a 1px Ink border and Ink text, same size and corners as the primary.
Stat card
Stat cards give hosts a quick read on how they're doing.
White background, 1.5rem padding, 12px corners, no shadow.
The number is big and bold (2rem, 700, Ink), with the label 0.5rem underneath (0.9rem, Slate).
One number per card. If you want to show two, that's two cards.
Format numbers like a person would read them: "₹12,400", "4.8 ★", "92%". Labels should be short and clear: "Total properties", "Avg. nightly rate".
Property cards and grid
This is where SpotStay really comes to life, and it's mostly down to the photos.
Cards are at least 320px wide with 2rem between them, dropping to a single column on mobile.
Same surface as stat cards: white with 12px corners. Photo first, then the title (Ink, 600) and details (Slate) underneath.
Use bright, real photos in landscape, and crop them all the same way (4:3 or 3:2 works well). A grid of mismatched crops looks messy fast.
Empty state
Empty states are often the very first thing a new host sees, so we put a bit of care into them.
White panel, 12px corners, everything centered, 4rem padding top and bottom, 2rem on the sides.
From top to bottom: a big icon (4rem), a title (1.5rem, Ink), one line of explanation (Slate), and the primary button.
Keep the copy encouraging and point to the next step. For example: "No properties yet" and then "Add your first place to start welcoming guests."
Dashboard header
Page title (2.5rem, 600, Ink) with a one-line subtitle underneath (1rem, Slate).
The primary button sits on the right, lined up with the top of the title.
Embedded content
Anything from outside SpotStay (maps, calendars, booking widgets) goes in .iframe-section: full width, 2rem above and below.
Where you can, wrap it in a white card with 12px corners. Third-party widgets can look a bit foreign otherwise, and this helps them blend in.
Shape, elevation & motion
We only use two corner sizes, almost no shadows, and one gentle transition. Keeping this list short is a big part of why the product feels consistent.
Token
Value
What it's for
--radius-sm
8px
Buttons, inputs, chips and other small controls
--radius-md
12px
Cards, panels, empty states, embeds
--transition-base
0.2s ease
Every hover, press and state change
--lift-hover
translateY(-1px)
The little lift on buttons

An easy way to remember the radii: small things get 8px, containers get 12px.
Shadows (or rather, the lack of them)
Our surfaces are flat. Cards stand out because they're white on grey, not because they're floating. The exception is things that genuinely sit above the page, like dropdown menus, modals or a property card you're hovering over. For those, use one soft shadow — 0 6px 16px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.12) — and please don't stack shadows or make them darker.
Motion
Animation should feel like a quick, polite nod, not a performance.
Keep transitions at 0.2s with ease. Skip bounces, spins and slow fades.
Hover can lift something by 1px, and it should settle back when clicked.
Some people get motion sick or simply prefer less movement, so always respect their system setting:
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  * { transition: none !important; transform: none !important; }
}
Responsive behavior
We have just one breakpoint, at 768px. Below that, everything stacks into a single column and the spacing tightens up a bit. One breakpoint keeps things predictable for both designers and engineers.
What changes
Desktop (above 768px)
Mobile (768px and below)
